5. Requirements, Risks & Constraints
5.1 Functional Requirements
•	Daily transactional data capture (date, item, quantity sold, price, sale/return flag).
•	Generate a per-item forecast ahead of each of the three weekly replenishment cycles.
•	Display forecast, confidence range, and suggested order range on the dashboard.
•	Let managers log the actual order placed against the forecast, to support later accuracy review.
5.2 Non-Functional Requirements
•	Forecast available at least 12 hours before each replenishment cycle.
•	Dashboard usable by non-technical staff without training documentation.
•	Data refresh automated, with minimal manual intervention.
•	Pilot explicitly scoped to the flagship store's data; not assumed to generalize to the other two stores without validation.
5.3 Key Risks & Constraints
•	Data quality: the raw data mixes sales and returns in a single field and lacks a store identifier, requiring careful handling in Module 3.
•	Project timeline: The limited timeframe available to complete the capstone restricts the depth of implementation, testing, and evaluation that can be achieved within the project lifecycle.
•	Generalization: a model trained on one store's data may not transfer cleanly to the other two stores at expansion.
•	Model drift: seasonal shifts in vegetable demand will require periodic re-validation, not a one-time model build.
These risks mirror common limitations flagged in the perishable-demand-forecasting literature, particularly regarding data quality and the need for ongoing model revalidation rather than a single, static deployment (Kaizer et al., 2022).
5.4 Ethical Risk Register
Risk	Likelihood	Impact	Mitigation
Category bias resulting in under-forecasting of lower-volume vegetables	Medium	Medium	Per-category accuracy audits, not just aggregate error.
Over-reliance on forecasts reducing managerial judgment	Medium	Medium	Frame all outputs as advisory; keep an override log; review it in governance meetings.
Unauthorized access to sensitive supplier pricing information	Low	Medium	Restrict wholesale price data to authorized analytics roles.
Model drift causing waste or stockouts	Medium	High	Scheduled re-validation each pilot phase; documented rollback plan.

6. Quality, Documentation & Governance
6.1 Documentation & Standards
Throughout the analytics lifecycle, project documentation will be maintained to support transparency, traceability, and future maintenance. Business documentation will include the Vision Document, project decisions, and stakeholder requirements. Technical documentation will include a data dictionary, model cards, repository documentation (README), and a decision log  capturing key modeling and implementation decisions. All project deliverables will be in NXU formatting and citation standards and will reflect professional documentation practices expected of real-world analytics projects.
6.2 Versioning Strategy
•	Code: Git Flow with main, develop, and feature branches, and pull requests and code reviews before changes are merged into the main branch.
•	Models: Semantic Versioning (vMAJOR.MINOR.PATCH), with each trained model accompanied by a model card documenting the training dataset, parameters, evaluation metrics, and release date.
•	Data: Immutable, date-stamped snapshots of processed datasets stored under data/processed/, ensuring that every forecast and model version can be traced back to the exact dataset used for training and evaluation.
6.3 Data Governance Principles
•	Access to raw and processed data is limited to the project team named in Section 4.
•	Wholesale price data is treated as confidential and is not exposed on the manager-facing dashboard.
•	Raw data is retained for the duration of the pilot and archived (not deleted) at pilot close, pending a decision on expansion.
References
1.	Jens Huber and Heiner Stuckenschmidt. 2020. Daily retail demand forecasting using machine learning with emphasis on calendric special days. International Journal of Forecasting. Volume 36. Issue 4. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ijforecast.2020.02.003
2.	João Victor Kaizer, Rosangela Cristina Tavares de Souza, and Leandro Barros Ribeiro Aylon. 2022. Quantitative models for forecasting demand for perishable products: A systematic review. Proceedings of the Ibero-American Conference on Artificial Intelligence. https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-031-20891-6_31
3.	Rakesh Priyadarshi, Abhijit Panigrahi, Satyabrata Routroy, and G. K. Garg. 2019. Demand forecasting at retail stage for selected vegetables: A performance analysis. Journal of Modelling in Management. Volume 14. Issue 4. https://doi.org/10.1108/JM2-01-2019-0005
4.	YAPWH. Updated 2022. Supermarket Sales Data. Kaggle. https://www.kaggle.com/datasets/yapwh1208/supermarket-sales-data






